import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Retro — spike Q0: can catopt's language be handl data?","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Retro — spike Q0: can catopt's language be handl data?","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/handl-q0-language-as-data.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/handl-q0-language-as-data.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/handl-q0-language-as-data.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-—-spike-q0-can-catopt-s-language-be-handl-data" tabindex="-1">Retro — spike Q0: can catopt&#39;s language be handl data? <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-—-spike-q0-can-catopt-s-language-be-handl-data" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro — spike Q0: can catopt&#39;s language be handl data?”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Plan</strong>: 0029 (the handl ↔ catopt boundary spike), <strong>Q0</strong>. <strong>Verdict</strong>: <strong>PASS</strong>, with two representational requirements (both fixed) and one named residue.</p><p><strong>Evidence</strong>: catopt <code>8d5b47d</code> (<code>lab/handl-spike/dump_fragments.py</code> + the generated <code>fragments/</code>), the emitter fix in <code>8de8cb5</code>; handl <code>b20b978a</code> (<code>crates/handl-kit-data-canonical/tests/catopt_fragments.rs</code></p><ul><li><code>tests/data/catopt-fragments/</code>).</li></ul><h2 id="the-question" tabindex="-1">The question <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-question" aria-label="Permalink to “The question”">​</a></h2><p>ADR 0010 claims handl <em>is</em> the kernel — catopt&#39;s vocabulary should be handl data, not merely similar to it. Q0 is the cheapest decisive test: encode one catopt law as a <code>TransformDef</code> (+ the shapes it needs), load it through handl&#39;s <code>language_from_dir</code>, and check it survives.</p><h2 id="method" tabindex="-1">Method <a class="header-anchor" href="#method" aria-label="Permalink to “Method”">​</a></h2><ol><li>Compare catopt&#39;s emitted rows (<code>catopt_discovery.handl</code>) against handl&#39;s actual types, field by field — <code>TransformDef</code>, <code>TransformRule</code>, <code>RewriteStep</code>, <code>TransformCondition</code>, <code>AstShapeDef</code>.</li><li>Materialize the seam: <code>lab/handl-spike/dump_fragments.py</code> writes catopt&#39;s vocabulary into the <strong>ten-file layout</strong> <code>language_from_dir</code> reads (eight required shell files + <code>transforms.yaml</code>).</li><li>Load it from Rust (handl&#39;s test) and assert the merge + the fields.</li></ol><h2 id="result-—-pass" tabindex="-1">Result — PASS <a class="header-anchor" href="#result-—-pass" aria-label="Permalink to “Result — PASS”">​</a></h2><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>test catopt_fragment_dir_assembles_a_language ... ok</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>test catopt_law_survives_as_data ... ok</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A <code>Language</code> assembles from catopt&#39;s data; the law is retrievable by id (<code>transform.catopt.assoc_add</code>), its <code>pass</code>/<code>node_kind</code> are intact, and its folded <code>when</code> deserializes as <code>And([KindAt, ChildCount])</code>.</p><h3 id="field-by-field-everything-matched-except-two-spellings" tabindex="-1">Field-by-field: everything matched except two spellings <a class="header-anchor" href="#field-by-field-everything-matched-except-two-spellings" aria-label="Permalink to “Field-by-field: everything matched except two spellings”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>catopt emits</th><th>handl expects</th><th>verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>id</code> · <code>pass</code> · <code>node_kind</code></td><td>same</td><td>✅</td></tr><tr><td><code>rule: {Declarative: {target_kind, children}}</code></td><td><code>TransformRule::Declarative</code></td><td>✅</td></tr><tr><td><code>KindAt{index,kind}</code> · <code>ChildCount(n)</code></td><td><code>TransformCondition</code> variants</td><td>✅</td></tr><tr><td><code>KeepPath([...])</code> · <code>Create{kind, children}</code></td><td><code>RewriteStep</code> variants</td><td>✅</td></tr><tr><td><code>children: [{role: arg0}, …]</code> · <code>payload_schema</code></td><td><code>AstShapeDef</code> fields</td><td>✅</td></tr><tr><td>the <code>catopt:</code> provenance block</td><td>no such field</td><td>✅ (serde ignores unknown fields)</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>when: [clause, …]</code> (a list)</strong></td><td><code>Option&lt;TransformCondition&gt;</code> — <strong>one</strong></td><td>❌ → fold into <code>And([...])</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>{&quot;Declarative&quot;: …}</code> (JSON map)</strong></td><td><strong><code>!Declarative</code> (YAML tag)</strong></td><td>❌ → emit tags</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="the-two-requirements-both-now-satisfied" tabindex="-1">The two requirements, both now satisfied <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-two-requirements-both-now-satisfied" aria-label="Permalink to “The two requirements, both now satisfied”">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong><code>when</code> is a single condition.</strong> handl&#39;s field is <code>Option&lt;TransformCondition&gt;</code>; a conjunction is spelled <code>And([...])</code>. catopt emitted a bare clause list, which does not deserialize. Fixed in the emitter (<code>8de8cb5</code>): omitted when empty, the clause itself when there is one, <code>And([...])</code> otherwise.</li><li><strong>Enum variants are YAML tags.</strong> Under <code>serde_yaml</code> a variant is <code>!Declarative</code>/<code>!Create</code>/<code>!KeepPath</code>/<code>!KindAt</code>/<code>!And</code>; under <code>serde_json</code> it is a map. <code>language_from_dir</code> reads YAML, so the dump tool emits tags (measured failure: <em>&quot;invalid type: map, expected a YAML tag starting with &#39;!&#39;&quot;</em>). Fixed in the dump tool.</li></ol><h3 id="named-residue-not-exercised-here" tabindex="-1">Named residue (not exercised here) <a class="header-anchor" href="#named-residue-not-exercised-here" aria-label="Permalink to “Named residue (not exercised here)”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>The leaf step has no counterpart.</strong> catopt renders a literal leaf as <code>{&quot;catopt:leaf&quot;: …}</code>; handl&#39;s <code>RewriteStep</code> has no such variant. <code>assoc_add</code> is structural, so it never arises — but any law with a constant on the rhs would need a mapping (candidate: a <code>Create</code>d node carrying the value in its <code>payload</code>, or <code>Fresh(hint)</code> for variables).</li><li><strong>The shell is a stub.</strong> catopt supplies <code>ast.yaml</code> (op shapes) and <code>transforms.yaml</code> (laws) of the ten files; the eight required shell files are empty today, because catopt&#39;s &quot;syntax&quot; is the IR, not a text language. A <em>real</em> catopt language would need <code>eval</code>/<code>types</code> entries — which is precisely ADR 0010&#39;s &quot;interpretations&quot; question, not Q0&#39;s.</li></ul><h2 id="what-q0-does-not-prove" tabindex="-1">What Q0 does <strong>not</strong> prove <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-q0-does-not-prove" aria-label="Permalink to “What Q0 does not prove”">​</a></h2><ul><li><s><strong>That a rewrite fires.</strong></s> <strong>Measured later the same night</strong> (plan 0030 step 1): a catopt law <em>does</em> fire through handl&#39;s <code>RewriteEngine</code> — one rewrite, and the result is <code>add(add(a, b), c)</code>, which is exactly what catopt&#39;s own <code>EGraph.apply_rule</code> produces for the same term (handl <code>82194954</code>). What remains unproven is only the <em>peer</em>: nothing in catopt calls handl yet.</li><li><strong>Anything about tensor values.</strong> That is Q1 (a host function carrying a tensor), and it is the question ADR 0010&#39;s &quot;host functions&quot; claim actually rests on.</li><li><strong>That the load path is reachable from the CLI.</strong> It is not: <code>hnl</code> loads kits through registered <em>emitters</em>; a data directory is reachable only through the <code>language_from_dir</code> API (or a small host that calls it). <code>FragmentSource::DataFile</code> is modeled but returns <code>LangError::NotImplemented</code> — that is the <em>runtime</em> half of the seam, and it is Q3&#39;s business.</li></ul><h2 id="cost" tabindex="-1">Cost <a class="header-anchor" href="#cost" aria-label="Permalink to “Cost”">​</a></h2><p>Hours, not days — because <code>hnl</code> is already installed and the load path already exists. No Rust code was written on the catopt side; the only handl-side change is a test.</p><hr><h2 id="addendum-2026-10-14-—-the-full-bank-the-pass-is-qualified" tabindex="-1">Addendum (2026-10-14) — the FULL bank: the PASS is qualified <a class="header-anchor" href="#addendum-2026-10-14-—-the-full-bank-the-pass-is-qualified" aria-label="Permalink to “Addendum (2026-10-14) — the FULL bank: the PASS is qualified”">​</a></h2><p>The verdict above rests on a <strong>one-law slice</strong> (<code>assoc_add</code>, purely structural). Loading the <strong>whole shipped bank</strong> — all 83 laws, 161 KB of <code>transforms.yaml</code> — tells a different story:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>transforms.yaml: .[14].rule.children[1]: invalid type: map,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>expected a YAML tag starting with &#39;!&#39;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Row 14&#39;s rule has a <strong>leaf</strong> on its right-hand side. catopt spells a leaf <code>{&quot;catopt:leaf&quot;: …}</code> (its own extension — handl&#39;s child vocabulary has no literal-leaf step, which the first version of this retro recorded as a <em>residue</em>); the bridge emits a map there, handl expects a tag, and the load fails.</p><p><strong>So the honest statement is:</strong></p><blockquote><p>catopt&#39;s vocabulary is expressible as handl data <strong>for structural laws</strong>. Leaf-bearing laws need a mapping first.</p></blockquote><p>Candidates for that mapping, both using vocabulary handl already has:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>leaf</th><th>handl step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a <strong>variable</strong></td><td><code>Fresh(hint)</code> — &quot;allocate a fresh <code>var</code> leaf with a unique, non-colliding name&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>a <strong>constant</strong></td><td><code>Create { kind: &lt;const-kind&gt;, payload: &lt;value&gt;, children: [] }</code> — provided the shape table declares a const kind</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both require the shape table to name the kinds involved, which is a <em>data</em> change (the direction ADR 0010 already sets), not an engine one.</p><p><strong>The gap is pinned, not hidden</strong>: handl&#39;s <code>catopt_full_bank_leaf_gap_is_pinned</code> asserts the failure and its shape, and panics with the transform count if the bank ever starts loading — i.e. it flips when the mapping lands. Fixture: <code>crates/handl-kit-data-canonical/tests/data/catopt-fragments-all/</code> (generated by <code>lab/handl-spike/dump_fragments.py --all</code>).</p><p><strong>Cost of the correction:</strong> one extra measurement (the full-bank load), and it changes the spike&#39;s read on Q0 from &quot;expressible&quot; to &quot;expressible for the structural majority; one named mapping stands between the bank and the kernel&quot;. That is a better answer than a PASS that only held for the case that was easiest to pass.</p><hr><h2 id="closure-2026-10-14-—-the-gap-is-shut-the-whole-bank-loads" tabindex="-1">Closure (2026-10-14) — the gap is shut; the whole bank loads <a class="header-anchor" href="#closure-2026-10-14-—-the-gap-is-shut-the-whole-bank-loads" aria-label="Permalink to “Closure (2026-10-14) — the gap is shut; the whole bank loads”">​</a></h2><p>The leaf mapping landed, and the bank revealed <strong>one more</strong> gap behind it: handl keys a rule by <code>pass:node_kind:priority</code>, and catopt has many laws per root kind, so they collided (<code>comm_add</code> vs <code>assoc_add</code>). Both are now closed on the producer&#39;s side:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>gap</th><th>mapping</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>leaf (<code>{&quot;catopt:leaf&quot;: …}</code> — a map where serde wants a tag)</td><td><code>!Fresh</code> for an unbound pattern variable; <code>!Create</code> of a declared <code>catopt:const</code> / <code>catopt:var</code> / <code>catopt:data</code> kind, with the data in the payload</td></tr><tr><td>rule collision (<code>pass:node_kind:priority</code>)</td><td>distinct priorities per <code>(pass, node_kind)</code> — a <em>load-time disambiguator</em>, since catopt&#39;s saturation is unordered</td></tr></tbody></table><p>handl&#39;s <code>catopt_full_bank_loads</code> (the pinned gap, flipped) asserts <strong>83 laws</strong> in <code>lang.transforms</code> and that the three leaf kinds are declared. Catopt <code>5ca8aef</code>; handl <code>9372bd32</code>.</p><p><strong>So Q0&#39;s verdict is now unqualified:</strong> catopt&#39;s whole law bank is expressible as handl language data and loads into a <code>Language</code> — not just the one-law slice the first measurement used.</p><p>Two lessons worth keeping, both about <em>where</em> a gap lives:</p><ol><li><strong>The first gap hid the second.</strong> Fixing the leaf took one edit; the collision only appeared once the leaf stopped failing first. A bank is not a slice, and a pinned-gap test is what makes the second one visible instead of mysterious.</li><li><strong><code>priority</code> is the interesting one.</strong> It is the first place where handl&#39;s schema demanded something catopt does not <em>have</em> (an order), and the honest answer was to supply a disambiguator and say plainly that it means nothing semantically. A future producer with a real ordering should map it to something that means something.</li></ol>`,41)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};